.TH PRINTBIGINT 3 "2026-09-21" "BigNums" "Library Functions Manual"
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
printBigInt, printBigFloat \- print a big number in decimal to standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqbignums.h\(dq
.sp
.BI "void printBigInt(const BigInt *" "a" ");"
.sp
.BI "void printBigFloat(const BigFloat *" "x" ","
.BI "    int " "decimal_places" ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B printBigInt()
writes the unsigned integer a in decimal to stdout.
.B printBigFloat()
writes x with a sign if negative, an integer part, a decimal point, and
fractional digits.  Negative decimal_places is treated as zero.
Even zero decimal places prints a decimal point: exact 2 prints "2.".
.PP
Neither function modifies its input or appends a newline.
The caller may use putchar to terminate a line.
.SH RETURN VALUE
These functions return no value and cannot report output or arithmetic errors.
.SH BUGS
The float printer has only a 22-byte fractional digit buffer.  Large
place counts can overrun it; do not use it for arbitrary-length decimal
output.  Modest counts such as 6 are used in the demo.
.PP
The routine attempts fractional rounding by adding 0.5, but prints the
integer part first and does not carry a rounded fraction into it.
Internal arithmetic errors are ignored; large magnitudes and scales can
produce invalid output.  Requested decimal places do not guarantee accuracy.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bignums (3),
.BR bignums (7)
